# UnboxingEngine

UnboxingEngine is an experimental C++17 scene and component engine for graphics,
collision, and computational rendering experiments. It combines an SDL2/OpenGL
renderer with scene transforms, reusable mesh primitives, event-driven input,
collision components, custom GLSL shaders, and floating-point render-to-texture
workflows.

The project is primarily a research and learning codebase. Its examples cover
interactive collision visualization, octree traversal, voxelization, radiosity,
custom shader passes, and iterative texture-based field calculations.

## Features

- Scene graph transforms through `CSceneNode` and `CSceneComposite`.
- Component-based rendering, collision, input, and update behavior.
- Deterministic rendering in scene-element registration order.
- SDL2 window/input integration and an OpenGL 3.2 core-profile renderer.
- GLSL 150 custom vertex and fragment shaders.
- Built-in cube, quad, sphere, torus, line, and other mesh helpers.
- Perspective, orthographic, and screen-ray camera operations.
- Named multiple-texture shader inputs.
- `RGBA32F` off-screen render targets.
- Overwrite, additive, and alpha render-target blend modes.
- Configurable per-target clearing for accumulation and iterative calculations.
- GPU texture statistics, snapshots, and render-step comparisons.
- Primitive, mesh, 2D component, ray, and octree collision algorithms.

## Requirements

- CMake 3.21 or newer.
- A C++17 compiler.
- A desktop OpenGL implementation supporting the 3.2 core profile.
- Git submodules initialized for SDL2, GoogleTest, and the other vendored
  dependencies.

Initialize dependencies after cloning an existing checkout:

```bash
git submodule update --init --recursive
```

Alternatively, clone with `--recurse-submodules`.

## Build

Configure and build the engine and examples:

```bash
cmake -S . -B build
cmake --build build -j
```

Build a specific example when iterating on one feature:

```bash
cmake --build build --target coctree_torus_minimal
./build/Examples/coctree_torus_minimal
```

On multi-configuration generators, build with a configuration such as
`--config Debug`; the executable is normally placed in a matching subdirectory
such as `build/Examples/Debug/`.

## Minimal Scene

The engine owns its systems while scene objects own their components and mesh
data. Register objects after `Start()`, and destroy their render resources before
`Release()`:

```cpp
#include "MeshPrimitivesUtils.h"
#include "SceneCompositeLibrary/BasicSceneCompositeTypes.h"
#include "UnboxingEngine.h"

using namespace unboxing_engine;

int main() {
    CCore engine(1000, 700, 32);

    Camera camera(1000, 700, 60.0f, 0.1f, 1000.0f);
    camera.SetPosition({0.0f, 0.0f, 8.0f});
    engine.SetCamera(camera);
    engine.Start();

    if (engine.IsRunning()) {
        auto mesh = primitive_utils::Sphere(2.0f);
        CSimpleMeshWireFrame sphere(*mesh);
        sphere.SetMaterial(redMaterial());

        engine.RegisterSceneElement(sphere);
        engine.Run();
        engine.UnregisterSceneElement(sphere);
    }

    engine.Release();
    return 0;
}
```

`Run()` processes input, update listeners, collision work, and rendering until
the application exits. `StepRender()` performs one controlled frame and is
useful for tests and texture diagnostics.

## Architecture

| Area | Main types | Responsibility |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Engine lifecycle | `CCore` | Owns systems, registers scene elements, and drives frame processing. |
| Scene graph | `CSceneNode`, `CSceneComposite` | Stores local/world transforms and attached components. |
| Events | `CEventDispatcher`, `UListener` | Connects input, update, collision, and lifecycle callbacks. |
| Rendering | `IRenderSystem`, `IRenderComponent` | Defines backend-independent render resources and component behavior. |
| OpenGL backend | `COpenGLRenderSystem` | Manages SDL, shaders, buffers, textures, framebuffers, and draw calls. |
| Collision | `CollisionSystem`, collider components | Evaluates registered colliders and dispatches intersection events. |
| Geometry | `CMeshBuffer`, `primitive_utils` | Stores indexed meshes and creates common primitives. |
| Diagnostics | `IRenderDebug` | Reads textures and calculates channel and frame-difference statistics. |

Scene elements render in `RegisterSceneElement()` order. This is significant for
multi-pass rendering: register producer passes before consumers that sample
their destination textures.

## Rendering And Shaders

The current backend requests OpenGL 3.2 core, so custom shaders use GLSL 150
core. The renderer supplies a uniform when the shader declares its expected
name:

- `u_model_matrix`
- `u_view_matrix`
- `u_projection_matrix`
- `u_camera_world_position`
- `u_camera_far`
- `color`
- `u_visualization_scale`

Texture samplers are bound by the names passed to `SetSrcTexture()` or
`SetTexture()`.

Rendering components serve distinct roles:

- `CDefaultMeshRenderComponent` renders geometry with the default shader.
- `CustomShaderMeshRenderComponent` owns custom vertex and fragment shaders.
- `RenderToTextureComponent` renders into an `RGBA32F` texture.
- `RenderTextureComponent` samples one or more existing textures for a later
  pass or final presentation.

### Render-Target Behavior

`RenderToTextureComponent` exposes two independent settings:

```cpp
component.SetRenderTargetClearEnabled(false);
component.SetRenderTargetBlendMode(
    systems::ERenderTargetBlendMode::Overwrite);
```

Use the blend modes according to the calculation:

| Mode | OpenGL behavior | Typical use |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `Overwrite` | Blending disabled | Full-screen simulation and ping-pong updates. |
| `Additive` | `source + destination` | Multiple geometry contributions accumulated in one target. |
| `Alpha` | Conventional source-alpha blending | Compositing visual layers. |

`RenderToTextureComponent` defaults to additive blending for compatibility with
the volume-accumulation path. Iterative shaders that already return the complete
next state should select `Overwrite`. Disabling clearing preserves pixels not
written by the pass; it does not by itself change blending.

Do not sample a texture while rendering into that same texture. Iterative
algorithms should use separate source and destination textures and swap or
cross-bind them between steps.

## Render Debugging

The render-debug API can inspect `RGBA32F` and `RGBA8U` textures and report:

- Per-channel minimum, maximum, sum, average, and nonzero average.
- Positive, negative, zero, finite, NaN, and infinity counts.
- Pixel locations and bounds for extrema and nonzero regions.
- Raw RGBA snapshots.
- Signed and absolute differences between render steps.
- Changed-pixel percentage and bounds.

Run the complete example:

```bash
cmake --build build --target render_debug_example
./build/Examples/render_debug_example
```

See [Render Debugging](documentation/RenderDebug.md) for API examples, epsilon
semantics, pixel addressing, and an iterative-field diagnostic workflow.

Texture readback is synchronous and may stall for pending GPU work. It is meant
for diagnostics rather than continuous release-frame instrumentation.

## Examples

| Target | Purpose |
| --- | --- |
| `2d_collision_vectors` | Interactive segment and box collision visualization. |
| `circle_triangle_collision` | Circle/sphere and triangle collision experiment. |
| `coctree_torus_minimal` | Minimal torus render with `COctree` construction. |
| `octree_ray_picking_lab` | Screen-ray picking with octree traversal visualization. |
| `bresenham_voxelization_port` | Legacy mesh ray sweep and voxel filling port. |
| `radiosity_port` | Patch radiosity and rasterized hemisphere form-factor experiment. |
| `atom_simulation` | Multi-pass floating-point texture and field-shader experiment. |
| `render_debug_example` | Texture statistics and consecutive-snapshot comparison. |

The detailed build commands, controls, self-tests, and algorithm notes are in
the [examples guide](Examples/README).

## Tests

Tests are optional at configure time:

```bash
cmake -S . -B build -DUNBOXING_ENGINE_BUILD_TESTS=ON
cmake --build build --target \
  unboxing_engine_tests \
  unboxing_engine_legacy_collision_algorithm_tests \
  unboxing_engine_collision_algorithm_tests \
  unboxing_engine_coctree_tests \
  unboxing_engine_mesh_primitive_tests
ctest --test-dir build --output-on-failure
```

The separate `unboxing_engine_minimal_unit_tests` target contains older test
code and is not part of the registered CTest suite.

## Project Layout

```text
include/                 Public engine headers
source/                  Core, component, collision, and debug implementations
sdl_gl_render_system/    SDL2/OpenGL render and input backend
Examples/                Maintained examples and experimental drafts
tests/                   GoogleTest targets
documentation/           Focused subsystem documentation
Legacy/                  Historical source retained for reference
third_party/             Vendored libraries and Git submodules
```

## Current Scope

The engine is under active experimental development rather than a stable SDK.
APIs can change as rendering and simulation workflows are generalized. In
particular:

- The root build currently selects the SDL/OpenGL backend directly.
- Render-target dimensions are taken from the camera when the component is
  initialized; existing textures are not automatically resized later.
- Render resources must be released before their owning render system is
  destroyed.
- Registration order is the current pass-order mechanism; there is no render
  graph or dependency scheduler.

